ns or  roster construction or game time  decisions  etc etc and so being able to  adequately explain your results to  different layers of people  again if you were explaining to someone  to a teammate your analysis  would not include the same kind of  details and context as if you were  explaining that  to an assistant coach for example which  um  ties perfectly into something that mitch  said we did not even coordinate this but  i was so happy when i saw this on one of  his slides i even wrote down  if you can't clearly explain your work  don't expect a decision maker to buy in  and use it  which is really so true and it is a true  essential skill to be able to  again distill your analysis and either  apply or remove  context and technical details based on  the audience that you're  trying to present to because without  again if you can't convince  say your coach on the results of you  know your analysis on timeouts  then your analysis is basically  pointless most of us are doing this work  in order to you know provide the data  behind these  data driven decisions and hopefully make  change  now we can't um as i said this is a  really essential  skill to have unfortunately we can't go  super in depth into how to develop that  skill because i was only given  uh 10 minutes for this talk and i'm not  a magician but  i'm hoping to convince you again today  that our markdown is a really useful  tool  to use that skill  so many of us are probably you've either  been at this stage of a workflow  or you know someone who's in the stage  or hopefully maybe you've helped someone  who's in the stage  who's using excel for their data  analysis  i mean i'm never someone who uh  i guess kind of who poo poos like excel  like excel is a great tool  and being proficient in excel and using  it proficiently is a really essential  skill i would think for a lot of data  analysis roles particularly if you work  closely  with people who might use excel for you  know again certain appropriate  business cases and might have less  technical roles than you do  but a lot of a lot of people do take  kind of the capabilities of excel  a little too far when i think they could  really be better served by using a tool  like r tidy verse all of its associated  packages etc  for you know making their data the  technical side of their data analysis  work  more reproducible so hopefully  again people move to this next step  which is an amazing step  again just taking the data analysis and  making that more reproducible  using our is amazing i personally stayed  at this step  for a very long time and again this is  miles of  this mile's better than uh this first  step i showed  but i think some people can stay in this  step too long as i said i did  where i had really was really pleased  with all the efficiency gains i got in  moving  my analysis work into r but i was still  handling kind of all the data  communication pieces  by kind of keeping all my documentation  and word  documents and creating all my slide  decks to you know  convey the results of my analyses to you  know various higher up audiences  in powerpoint and so what i'm hoping to  convince people  is actually the ideal workflow is to  substitute  our markdown for all these kind of data  science communication  names because there are so many output  formats different  that are possible with our markdown and  keeping everything in the same universe  in the same ecosystem will kind of  streamline the communication side  of your data science work just like  using our tidy words etc really  streamlines the analysis part  of your data analysis work so we talked  about  how you know you can you need to  communicate at different levels  and many of you might be familiar with  kind of what i call like a classic our  markdown  document which doesn't take any kind of  special package it outputs an html file  these can be really useful again to  yourself and your teammates  as they really easily incorporate code  and plot  and text generally text that's more fl  it's more fleshed out than just you know  short comments in our script  um this example is actually a lab  assignment from my course but it's a  good example of how you can very easily  combine text and code  and plots into a simple html file that's  again very easy to share and it really  makes you focus  on reproducibility i make my students  use our markdown  because part of the class is learning  about how to get used to a reproducible  data science workflow  and if i cannot you know reproduce your  html file on  my computer you lose points um what you  know in the real world means your work  is not as reproducible  um so that is a great step and then also  in terms when you need to  you know start presenting to some layers  of people above you  like the if you if we stick to our  example of you have all of your  work that you did on on analyzing like  the values of timeouts and your  you know nfl coach wants this data  you're not going to send him hopefully  maybe there are some coaches out there  who would appreciate it but you're  probably not going to send him like your  really long  standard our markdown file that has all  of your different code decisions in your  plots like that's too much information  but thankfully our markdown has lots of  other options you can create  dashboards with flex dashboards you can  create slides with sharingan  these slides i've created today are  created again with our markdown with  sharon again  these particular slides are pre-played  because this talk is pretty short and  mostly  talking about theoretical things and not  necessarily showing codes and plots  but it's really easily really easy to  again incorporate code  and or plots and of course all the you  know images and other formatting things  that you would expect to have  from presentations but we can move away  from the land of having to screenshot  all of your plots and then pull those  into your powerpoint by